\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Comparison with the periodic quartic state}
\date{2026-11-09}

\p{Use mass-one units and the common site space of
\ref{fdip-00IB}. Put #{\kappa=\lambda'/6}.}

\p{\citet{Theorem 1.1(ii), arXiv v2, p. 3}{bauerschmidt2024logsobolev}
supplies the positive small-coupling threshold
##{\kappa_{\mathrm{BD}}=\kappa_{\mathrm{BD}}(2,1/2)>0.}
Assume
##{0<\kappa<\kappa_*:=
 \min\{\kappa_{\mathrm{BD}},(24B)^{-1/2}\}.}
The periodic comparison state #{\omega_{\mathrm{BD}}} is integration
against the normalized density with action
##{\mathcal A_{\mathrm{BD}}(\phi)
 =\tfrac12h_\epsilon(\phi,
       (-\covsq_{\epsilon,P}+r_{\mathrm{BD}}\bbI)\phi)
       +\tfrac\kappa4\|\phi\|_{L^4(\epsilon^2)}^4,
 \quad r_{\mathrm{BD}}=\tfrac12-3\kappa c_P+6\kappa^2b.}
For either law write #{\mathcal C_\omega(u,v)
=\omega(h_\epsilon(u,\phi)h_\epsilon(v,\phi))}.
On the positive cone of the common function space,
##{0\le\mathcal C_{\mathrm{HD}}(u,v)
      \le\mathcal C_{\mathrm{BD}}(u,v)
      \le K\|u\|_{L^1(\epsilon^2)}\|v\|_\infty
       \qquad(u,v\ge0),}
with #{K} independent of #{N,\ell}. In physical parameters the
coupling domain is the nonempty interval
##{m_0>0,\qquad 0<\lambda /m_0^2<\eta,
            \qquad\eta=6\kappa_*>0.}
The comparison is positivity on this function cone, not a claim of
Loewner order on arbitrary signed bilinear tests.}

\proof{
\p{Choose the site-indicator basis used in \ref{fdip-00HV}.
The whole-space Wick quartic has coefficient #{\lambda'/24=\kappa /4}.
Dropping only the constant term in the normalized density gives
#{r_{\mathrm{HD}}=1-3\kappa c_\epsilon^\infty(1)}.
Here #{c_\epsilon^\infty(s)} denotes the whole-lattice site
variance with squared mass #{s}; at #{s=1} it is #{c_\epsilon(1)}.
Equations (1.1)-(1.3) of
\citek{bauerschmidt2024logsobolev}, with its reference mass squared
equal to #{\mu=1/2}, give exactly the displayed #{r_{\mathrm{BD}}}.
Periodization of the positive whole-lattice kernel and its positive
heat-kernel resolvent give
##{c_P(1/2)\ge c_\epsilon^\infty(1/2)
                    \ge c_\epsilon^\infty(1).}
Thus \ref{fdip-00IB} implies
##{r_{\mathrm{HD}}-r_{\mathrm{BD}}
       \ge\tfrac12-6\kappa^2B\ge\tfrac14.}
}

\p{The killed and periodic Laplacians have the same diagonal
#{4/\epsilon^2} on these sites. Adding each wrap coupling keeps
that diagonal fixed and makes its off-diagonal coupling more
ferromagnetic, as in \ref{fdip-00HV}. First keep
#{r=r_{\mathrm{HD}}}. Each bond-strength derivative of
#{\mathbb E(\phi_x\phi_y)} is a positive multiple of
#{\operatorname{Cov}(\phi_x\phi_y,\phi_u\phi_v)}, nonnegative by
\citet{Theorem V.2, p. 207}{guerra1975euclideanII}.
Next lower #{r} to #{r_{\mathrm{BD}}}; then
##{\frac\partial{\partial r}\mathbb E(\phi_x\phi_y)
 =-\frac{\epsilon^2}{2}\sum_z
       \operatorname{Cov}(\phi_x\phi_y,\phi_z^2)\le0.}
Theorem V.1 of the same source gives nonnegative two-point functions.
These two monotone interpolations prove the sitewise comparison.}

\p{The negative counterterms do not invalidate this application.
Throughout the interpolation retain an arbitrary positive Gaussian
reference mass. The kinetic matrix is positive semidefinite by
diagonal dominance and nonnegative row sums; adding the reference
mass makes it positive definite with nonpositive off-diagonal
entries. Put the residual quadratic term into onsite weights
#{\exp(-a u^4+b u^2)}, #{a>0}. These are bounded, positive, even
and continuous, exactly the class in GRS Theorems V.1-V.3.
Finite quartic domination justifies differentiation in each
interpolation parameter.}

\p{The periodic law has, by Theorem 1.1(ii) of
\citek{bauerschmidt2024logsobolev}, a log-Sobolev constant
#{\gamma\ge K^{-1}>0} uniformly in #{\epsilon,L} on the stated
coupling interval. Its equation (1.12) gives
##{\chi:=\epsilon^2\sum_y\mathbb E_{\mathrm{BD}}(\phi_0\phi_y)
                         \le\gamma^{-1}\le K.}
Translation invariance of the comparison torus makes this a bound
for every row. The sitewise comparison therefore yields
#{\epsilon^2\sum_y\mathbb E_{\mathrm{HD}}(\phi_x\phi_y)\le K}
for every #{x}. Multiplying by the nonnegative site values of
#{u,v}, summing and using #{v\le\|v\|_\infty} proves the
stated bilinear inequalities. No bounded-square translation
invariance is used.}
}

\p{This combines the cited periodic estimate with the GRS
correlation inequalities for this half-Dirichlet state. It does not
identify two infinite-volume laws. The constant #{\gamma} concerns
stochastic dynamics and is not a relativistic mass gap.}
